   "text": "Your Databricks cost per job report is probably missing the jobs that cost the most.\n\nHere's why, straight from Databricks' own documentation.\n\nCost gets tied to a job through the job ID in the billing data. Jobs that run on All-Purpose clusters don't carry one. So Databricks' own cost-per-job queries leave them out.\n\nAnd All-Purpose is the most expensive way to run a scheduled job. On AWS Premium, it lists at $0.55 per DBU against $0.15 for job compute. That's 3.67x for the same work.\n\n5 steps to find and fix them:\n\n1. Spot the gap: check how much billed usage has no job ID\n2. Price the gap: compare the All-Purpose rate with job compute\n3. Find the jobs: flag tasks set to run on an existing all-purpose cluster\n4. Move them: point scheduled work at job compute or serverless jobs\n5. Report the rest honestly: untraced spend is a visibility gap, never a saving\n\nStep 4 is configuration, not a migration. It's often the easiest win on the whole bill.\n\nSwipe through for the details.\n\n#Databricks #FinOps #DataEngineering",
