   "text": "A Databricks cluster running at 15% CPU costs exactly the same as one running at 90%.\n\nClassic compute is billed on nodes and hours. Not on how busy those nodes are.\n\nSomebody sized a cluster for a peak months ago. The peak passed. The size didn't. It still runs that way every night.\n\nThe good news: Databricks already records what every node was doing, minute by minute, in system.compute.node_timeline.\n\nThe catch: it only keeps that data for 90 days.\n\nLook at two numbers per cluster, the average CPU and the 95th percentile, and you'll find three kinds:\n\n→ Oversized. Average under 20% and still under 40% at its peak. It needs fewer or smaller nodes.\n\n→ Spiky. Quiet most of the run, briefly flat out. It needs a real autoscaling range, not a smaller size.\n\n→ Busy. Working most of the time. Leave it alone and spend your effort elsewhere.\n\nAn average on its own can mislead you. A cluster that averages 18% because it hit 95% for four minutes needs a different fix from one that never goes above 30%.\n\nCheck your top 10 clusters by cost this week, before that data expires.\n\n#Databricks #CloudCost #FinOps",
   "first_comment": "This utilisation check is one of the steps in a free live masterclass by BigHammer.ai founder and CEO Srinath Reddy on reducing Databricks costs: Thursday 15 October, 12:00 PM ET / 5 PM UK. Bring your own cluster questions. Save a seat: https://webinar.bighammerai.com/",
   "sources": [
    "Databricks compute system tables docs: node_timeline minute-level utilisation, 90-day retention",
    "BigHammer email series Issue 6 (avg under 20%, p95 under 40%, 30+ minute runs)"
   ],
   "numbers_for_signoff": [
    "20% / 40% thresholds",
    "90 days"
   ]
